You can run Kali Linux inside Ubuntu using Docker by pulling the official kalilinux/kali-rolling image from Docker Hub. The base Docker image is minimal and comes with no pre-installed security tools, so you will need to install tool packages inside the container after launching it.

Step 1: Pull and Start Kali Linux

Make sure Docker is installed on your Ubuntu system, then download and launch the container.
• Pull the official Kali image. Download the latest rolling release from Docker Hub:bash
sudo docker pull kalilinux/kali-rolling
Use code with caution.
• Run an interactive container. Start the container with a name so you can easily return to it later without losing your installed tools:bash
sudo docker run -it --name my-kali kalilinux/kali-rolling /bin/bash
Use code with caution.

Step 2: Install Kali Tools Inside the Container

Once you are inside the Kali root@# prompt, update the package list and install the tools you need.
• Update package repositories. Refresh the APT cache before installing anything:bash
apt update && apt upgrade -y
Use code with caution.
• Install individual tools. Add specific utilities like Nmap, Metasploit, or SQLMap to keep the container lightweight:bash
apt install -y nmap sqlmap metasploit-framework
Use code with caution.
• Install a full Kali toolset (optional). Use Kali metapackages if you want the standard suite of penetration testing tools (this requires several gigabytes of disk space):bash
apt install -y kali-linux-headless
Use code with caution.

Step 3: Manage Your Kali Container

Avoid using the --rm flag when running Kali so your installed tools stay saved when you exit.
• Exit the container. Type exit inside the Kali terminal to stop and leave the container.
• Restart your saved container. Bring your Kali environment back online anytime with its installed tools intact:bash
sudo docker start -ai my-kali
Use code with caution.
